      "id": 1,
      "text": "Sutherland Springs was founded in 1854 on the west bank of Cibolo Creek, originally granted to Manuel Tarín.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/sutherland-springs-tx",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Southern Pacific railroad that once served the town was abandoned in 1971, marking the end of an era of rail travel.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/sutherland-springs-tx",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1990, Pattillo Higgins razed the historic hotel and many other buildings to expand his Buffel grass production.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/sutherland-springs-tx",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "By 2016, Sutherland Springs still boasted two churches, a general store, a community center, a post office, and a local history museum.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/sutherland-springs-tx",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A local dairy operated on County Road 315 by Adele and Gus, who raised their children in the town’s rural heart.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
